# Subagent delegation

* Use subagents when they reduce wall-clock time or keep read-heavy context out of the main agent; parallelize independent work when useful. Do not force delegation for trivial tasks.
* Launch Task only with the latest Composer family and the latest Grok family. Resolve both from the live Task allowlist in this session: take the newest Composer version listed, including its Fast and non-Fast variants, and the newest Grok version listed, including its effort and speed variants. Do not use any other model family, and do not use an older Composer or Grok version when a newer one is listed. If the allowlist has no allowed slug for the chosen tier, skip that launch and report the miss; do not substitute a disallowed model.
* This rule is an explicit standing request to set Task `model` instead of `inherit` whenever the parent is not already the latest Composer or latest Grok resolved above. Choose the least expensive allowed model that can reliably complete the delegated task. Prefer the latest Composer; reserve the latest Grok for necessary cases. The following cost, speed, and intelligence tiers express user routing preferences, not fixed prices or latency guarantees:
  * Latest Composer Fast: extremely low cost, very high speed, lower intelligence; first choice for bounded, low-judgment, independently verifiable work such as symbol searches, evidence gathering, summaries, running specified checks, and mechanical edits. Use the `explore` type for codebase search; use `generalPurpose` with the same Composer Fast slug when Explore cannot take the work, including broader exploration, routine implementation, and analysis that still fits Fast.
  * Latest Composer non-Fast: medium cost, medium speed, higher intelligence; use for complex implementation, debugging, and reviews requiring substantial semantic judgment.
  * Latest Grok: extremely high cost, slower speed, very high intelligence; use sparingly for exceptionally difficult or ambiguous work when the latest Composer is unlikely to suffice, or its results reveal a concrete capability gap. Prefer the highest effort variant listed for that Grok version. Explain why Grok is necessary before spawning it.
* Select the model explicitly when spawning instead of accidentally inheriting a disallowed or more expensive parent model. Use a matching built-in Task type (`explore`, `cursor-guide`, `docs-researcher`, `ci-investigator`, review subagents, or `generalPurpose`) with an allowed model; a custom agent file is unnecessary for routing. Follow the live Task allowlist for model overrides and context forking, and provide a self-contained handoff; do not resume a prior agent unless the user asked to continue that agent.
* Choose reasoning effort to fit the task and supported model levels: low for straightforward work, medium for routine judgment, and high or above for complex logic or edge cases. Honor explicit user settings. Escalate from latest Composer Fast to latest Composer non-Fast to latest Grok when evidence warrants it; do not retry an unsuitable cheaper allowed model indefinitely.
* Give each subagent a bounded scope and require concise conclusions, file or source references, exact check commands and outcomes, changed-file summaries when applicable, and unresolved uncertainty. For edits, assign file ownership and tell agents they share the workspace and must preserve others' changes. Subagents must not expand scope or delegate further unless explicitly asked.
* Keep critical decisions and irreversible, security-sensitive, or privileged actions with the main agent. Review key evidence and changes instead of redoing all delegated work.
* Before delegating, tell the user the model and effort, scope, and expected evidence.
